0,Kurdish language - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kurdish_language,"Music. Mythology. Sport.Southern Kurdish (Pehlewani) is spoken in the Kermanshah, Ilam and Lorestan provinces of Iran and in the Khanaqin District of eastern Iraq.[28] Laki and Kordali (Palai) are often included in Southern Kurdish,[29] but they have some distinct features.[30].","Kurdish, Southern",Indo-European
1,Kurdish classical music - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kurdish_classical_music,"Kurdish classical music includes a variety of genres that reflect the cultural and historical diversity of Kurdish communities. Major categories include folk music, religious music, and instrumental compositions, each serving distinct social and artistic functions.","Kurdish, Southern",Indo-European
2,The Music of Kurdistan - Elbphilharmonie Mediatheque,https://www.elbphilharmonie.de/en/mediatheque/the-music-of-kurdistan/898,"Aug 31, 2023 · For one whole weekend, the Elbphilharmonie is celebrating the diversity and vitality of Kurdish music, with artists such as the singer Aynur and the kamancheh virtuoso Kayhan Kalhor.","Kurdish, Southern",Indo-European
3,Kurdish Voices from Iran & Iraq: Southern Kurdish languageKurdish folk music — GrokipediaSorani vs Kurmanji — A Guide to Kurdish Music's Two Great ...Kurdish classical music - WikipediaKurdish music — Grokipedia,https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=0_GX_S4EvKw,"Jan 14, 2026 · The Zerrûşî dialect is a variety of Southern Kurdish spoken by members of the Zerrûş tribe in parts of eastern Iraq and western Iran, particularly in and around Khanaqin, Kut, Baghdad, and Ilam. Kurdish diaspora communities, particularly in Europe and North America, have played a pivotal role in preserving and disseminating traditional folk music amid political restrictions in their homelands. Apr 25, 2026 · A clear guide to the two main Kurdish music traditions — Sorani (Central) from the south, Kurmanji (Northern) from the north. Differences in language, scales, ornaments, and famous artists. Kurdish classical music includes a variety of genres that reflect the cultural and historical diversity of Kurdish communities. Major categories include folk music, religious music, and instrumental compositions, each serving distinct social and artistic functions. These accolades underscore collaborations that have elevated Kurdish music within world music circuits, fostering performances and recordings that highlight its improvisational and narrative depth. Where does Kurdish music come from? Kurdish music isn't one tradition — it's two great rivers that share a source. The Sorani (Central Kurdish) tradition flows through Iraqi Kurdistan and parts of Iran. The Kurmanji (Northern Kurdish) tradition is the music of Turkish Kurdistan, Syrian Kurdistan, and Armenian Kurdistan. They share a heart but speak with different voices. Who sings in Northern Kurdish music? Northern Kurdish music is dominated by the dengbêj — storyteller-singers who carry epic narratives in their memory and voice. The dengbêj sings unaccompanied or with minimal instrumental support, often improvising over a fixed melody. What instruments are used in Kurdish music? Common instruments include the Daf, a frame drum used for complex rhythmic patterns; Dumbek (tambourine), a goblet-shaped hand drum; Bağlama, a long-necked lute; and Nay, an end-blown flute; Oud, a fretless lute with a deep tone, is also used for melodic accompaniment in Kurdish classical music. What language did Iraqi Kurdistan speak? The Yezidi oral tradition in Iraqi Kurdistan. 2001. ""However, it was the southern dialect of Kurdish, Sorani, the majority language of the Iraqi Kurds, which received sanction as an official language of Iraq."" ^ ""Kurdish language issue and a divisive approach"". Kurdish Academy of Language. Archived from the original on 17 October 2015.","Kurdish, Southern",Indo-European
